{
  "reviewer": "abstract_conclusion_consistency_auditor",
  "paper_id": "bicycle-infrastructure-paper",
  "run_status": "ok",
  "summary": "I compared the abstract, introduction and conclusion with the body and appendix. Most headline counts reconcile: 92 articles, 43 projects, 29 projects dated to day or month (19+10), 14 of 15 counties, and the 38 percent listing share. The summary sections overstate or leave out caveats the body states. (1) The 97 percent human–LLM agreement is stated as parity without the body's \"indicative only\" caveat. The sample has 3 relevant articles, and the LLM flagged 4. (2) The full-run counts are presented as findings, but the body says a random sample of the full run still needs checking against human coding. (3) The abstract says the data can be linked to the FINN.no listings, but the body says they have not been linked. (4) A single NVDB linkage is phrased as \"shows\". (5) The introduction and contribution claim scalable treatment data, but Next Steps lists hand-done steps and says several steps remain before causal use. (6) \"Only two actual openings\" is stale given Table 2 footnote c. (7) Body inconsistencies: the 2-of-34 (6 percent) article yield versus five relevant articles, the footnote 3 Stord coding versus Table 3, and the introduction's description of two coders as the LLM benchmark.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "CONSIST-001",
